We need to find how many combinations of entrees, drinks, and desserts satisfy these conditions:  - Total cost is $7.50 - total price = 7.50 dollars.  Let's compute it step by step.  The only constraint on each choice is that they must be less than or equal to $7.50. So we just count the number of ways to choose from each category.  For example, if Crystal chooses "pizza" for entree, then her options for drinks are lemonade or soda; for desserts, frozen yogurt or cookies. That gives us two choices per entree. But since there are three categories for each item, so total possibilities depend on the number of items available at each level. Let's list them out explicitly.  \[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Entree}\\
&\qquad pizza\\
&\qquad corn dog\\
&\qquad fish&amp;- chips\\
&\qquad fried rice
\end{aligned}
\]Each of these has four choices for drink, but some may not have a dessert option. For instance, corn dog doesn't have a dessert option. Thus, let's compute this systematically.  \[\boxed{\text{Step 1: Compute the number of choices}}\]\[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Total choices for entree}\\
&= 3 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 9
\end{aligned}
\]\[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Total choices for drink}\\
&= 4
\end{aligned}
\]\[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Total choices for dessert}\\
&= 2
\end{aligned}
\]Thus, total possible meals $= 9 \times 4 \times 2 = 72$.  But note that some combos might be invalid because an entree with no dessert would violate the condition. We check those cases later.  So final answer is $\boxed{72}$.  \[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Number of valid meals}\\
&= 72 - \text{invalid combos}\\
&= 72 - 6 = 66
\end{aligned}
\]\[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Invalid combos}\\
&= \text{combinations where entree has no dessert}\\
&= 3 \times 2 = 6
\end{aligned}
\]\[
\text{Final answer}\\
= 72 - 6 = \boxed{66}.
\end{aligned}
\]

